1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,342
Imagine you are tidying a desk.

2
00:00:02,342 --> 00:00:06,138
You do not throw every paper into one anonymous pile.

3
00:00:06,138 --> 00:00:11,938
You take a small box, write a name on the lid, and place a single note inside.

4
00:00:11,938 --> 00:00:14,644
A variable works in that same spirit.

5
00:00:14,644 --> 00:00:17,966
It is a named place in the computer's memory,

6
00:00:17,966 --> 00:00:22,126
reserved so your program can find a piece of information again.

7
00:00:22,126 --> 00:00:25,559
The name on the box is not the information.

8
00:00:25,559 --> 00:00:27,174
It is the handle.

9
00:00:27,174 --> 00:00:31,587
When we write the word score, we are pointing at a location,

10
00:00:31,587 --> 00:00:33,565
not at the number itself.

11
00:00:33,565 --> 00:00:35,907
Inside the box is the value.

12
00:00:35,907 --> 00:00:38,976
That is the information the name currently holds.

13
00:00:38,976 --> 00:00:43,864
At the beginning, score holds zero, because we have not counted anything yet.

14
00:00:43,864 --> 00:00:45,842
Now, here's the important part.

15
00:00:45,842 --> 00:00:47,252
Programs change.

16
00:00:47,252 --> 00:00:50,575
If we could not change what a box contains,

17
00:00:50,575 --> 00:00:54,261
we would need a new box for every new number,

18
00:00:54,261 --> 00:00:57,694
and we would forget where the latest number lives.

19
00:00:57,694 --> 00:00:59,104
So we reassign.

20
00:00:59,104 --> 00:01:01,082
We write score equals ten.

21
00:01:01,082 --> 00:01:04,515
We are not creating a second box labeled score.

22
00:01:04,515 --> 00:01:08,675
We are opening the same box and replacing the note inside.

23
00:01:08,675 --> 00:01:10,085
The name stays.

24
00:01:10,085 --> 00:01:11,699
The value can change.

25
00:01:11,699 --> 00:01:16,113
After the assignment, whenever the program asks for score, it receives ten,

26
00:01:16,113 --> 00:01:17,523
not zero.

27
00:01:17,523 --> 00:01:22,774
The visual is helpful: a lid you can read, and contents you can swap.

28
00:01:22,774 --> 00:01:24,388
It also has limits.

29
00:01:24,388 --> 00:01:27,094
Real memory is not a wooden crate.

30
00:01:27,094 --> 00:01:29,689
The computer stores bits at an address,

31
00:01:29,689 --> 00:01:34,213
and the name is a label your programming language keeps for you.

32
00:01:34,213 --> 00:01:38,626
Remember this: a variable binds a stable name to a replaceable value,

33
00:01:38,626 --> 00:01:44,426
so the rest of the program can talk about the thing without chasing a moving number.

34
00:01:44,426 --> 00:01:47,495
That is how a variable stores a value.
